# Running {{ model_name }} on a SLURM cluster

Three files, and only the first is meant to be edited:

| file | what it is |
|---|---|
| `config.sh` | site and run settings — partitions, account, paths, what to run before `meds-model`. **Edit this.** |
| `job.sbatch` | one job = one `meds-model` invocation. Stage-independent; resources arrive on the command line. |
| `submit.sh` | queues the whole chain with `--dependency` between stages, and optionally as a job array over tasks. |

## Quick start

```bash
$EDITOR slurm/config.sh          # MEDS_ROOT, LABELS_DIR, RUN_DIR, PARTITION_*, ACCOUNT, SETUP
./slurm/submit.sh --dry-run      # read the sbatch commands before anything is queued
./slurm/submit.sh
```

`--dry-run` is worth using every time the config changes: it prints exactly what would be submitted, so a
wrong partition or a typo'd path costs nothing instead of a queue slot and a failed job.

## What it submits

The stage list comes from `meds-model commands`, so this follows whatever DAG this model registers —
{% if profile == 'supervised' %}here `preprocess_data → supervised_train → predict`.
{% elif profile == 'finetune' %}here `preprocess_data → pretrain → supervised_train → predict`.
{% elif profile == 'probe' %}here `preprocess_data → pretrain → infer → supervised_train → predict`.
{% elif profile == 'zero_shot_direct' %}here `preprocess_data → pretrain → predict`.
{% elif profile == 'zero_shot_materialized' %}here `preprocess_data → pretrain → infer → predict`.
{% elif profile == 'packaged' %}here `preprocess_data → predict`.
{% else %}run `meds-model commands` to see it.
{% endif %}
Each stage becomes one job, chained with `--dependency=afterok`, so a failure stops the rest of the chain
rather than running the next stage against a missing artifact.

`preprocess_data` gets `PARTITION_CPU` and no GPU — it shells out to MEDS-transforms and meds-torch-data
and never builds a model. Everything else gets `PARTITION_GPU` and `--gres=gpu:$GPUS`. Set `GPUS=0` to put
the whole chain on CPU.

Artifacts land under `$RUN_DIR`:

```
$RUN_DIR/data/                       # patients/ — built once, shared by every task
$RUN_DIR/models/pretrained/          # task-free, so also built once (profiles with `pretrain`)
$RUN_DIR/<task>/models/supervised/   # per task
$RUN_DIR/<task>/predictions/         # per task
```

Job output goes to `slurm-logs/<job-name>-<jobid>.out`.

## Several tasks at once

A model is trained per task, not per horizon or per endpoint — the task lives in `external_labels_dir`. To
run a fan of them, list the label directories one per line and submit an array:

```bash
printf '%s\n' /labels/mortality_24h /labels/mortality_48h /labels/mortality_72h > tasks.txt
./slurm/submit.sh --tasks-file tasks.txt
```

The task-free stages (`preprocess_data`, and `pretrain` where the DAG has it) are still submitted **once**;
only the task-dependent ones become array elements, and they are chained with `--dependency=aftercorr`, so
task *N*'s `predict` waits on task *N*'s training rather than on all of them. `<task>` in the paths above
is the basename of each labels directory.

## Overrides

Anything after `--` is appended to every `meds-model` invocation as a Hydra override:

```bash
./slurm/submit.sh -- trainer.max_epochs=200 batch_size=64
```

For settings that should always apply on this cluster, use `OVERRIDES` in `config.sh` instead.

**`num_workers` is deliberately not derived from `$CPUS`.** Changing the worker count changes the order in
which the dataloader consumes the RNG, so two runs that differ only in `num_workers` are not comparable
even at the same seed. Set it once, on purpose, and record it next to your results.

## Reruns

Artifacts are immutable: a stage whose output directory already exists fails rather than overwriting it.
That is deliberate — it is what makes a directory that exists a directory that finished. To replace one,
either point `RUN_DIR` somewhere new or pass `-- do_overwrite=true`.

Individual stages can be re-run on their own once their inputs exist:

```bash
./slurm/submit.sh --only supervised_train -- do_overwrite=true
```

## Notes

- `submit.sh` runs `meds-model commands` on the login node, so `SETUP` has to work there too. If your
  cluster forbids that, run `--dry-run` on a compute node and submit the printed commands.
- Paths containing spaces are passed through correctly by `submit.sh`, but `OVERRIDES` is word-split by
  design (it is a list of Hydra overrides), so keep spaces out of it.
- `--dependency=aftercorr` requires the two arrays to have the same index range; that holds here because
  both come from the same task list.
